Take the bottom panel out of the layout while a phone shows the keyboard in landscape and the focus is not in it.

With the soft keyboard up on a phone held in landscape the page is about 122px tall, and 0033 puts the title bar and the status bar away so that the editor keeps four lines. An open panel undid that. It cannot shrink under its 77px minimum, so it kept its size and the editor was left with its tab strip and no line of code (measured on an API 37 emulator with a 411dp wide screen: the editor part 41px, 7px of it below the tab strip, the panel 69px). Running a file opens the terminal panel, so a tap in the editor afterwards raised the keyboard over an editor that showed nothing.

On Android, Layout#updateShortWindow now also takes the panel out of the grid at the moment the window turns short, and puts it back when the window is tall again (the same 200px and 240px as 0033). It applies to a panel above or below the editor that the layout state shows open, with the editor visible, and only when the panel does not hold the focus at that moment: a terminal the keyboard is typing into stays where it is, because hiding it would take the keyboard down with it. The decision is made when the window turns short or tall, so moving the focus between the editor and the panel while the keyboard stays up does not change it.

The panel goes before the grid is sized and comes back after it. Shown into a grid that is still short, a panel is squeezed to its minimum height, and the grid then gives the room the window gained to the editor, so a panel that was 218px tall came back at 69px (measured on an API 37 emulator with a 411dp wide screen, the keyboard raised and put away again). updateShortWindow takes a flag for the call after the grid layout: before it only a window turning short is acted on (the panel is hidden at the size it had, which is the size the grid remembers for it), after it only a window turning tall. The title bar and the status bar follow the same order.

The layout state still says the panel is open, so it is drawn again at its size when the window is tall. While it is out the part visibility listener skips the change the grid reports, which it would otherwise read as the user closing the panel, and `isVisible` answers false for it, so everything that asks treats it as hidden: a toggle, a focus request or running a file shows it instead of closing a panel nobody can see, a maximize or a move to another side reads the size the grid remembers for a hidden view (the live size of a view outside the grid is 0), and so does the size saved for the next start. Showing or hiding the panel ends the yield.

A panel that stays in a short window, because it holds the focus or because it was shown there, is squeezed to its minimum, and the grid gives the room the window gains to the editor. So the height the panel had when the window turned short is remembered, given back when the window is tall again, and saved in place of the squeezed one in the meantime.

Measured on an API 33 emulator (WebView 149, 411dp wide, landscape) with the keyboard up and the panel out: Ctrl+J used to do nothing the first time and show the panel at 97px, with the editor at 13px, the second time, and the panel was still 97px once the keyboard was away. Now the first press shows it, and a 178px panel is 179px after the keyboard goes away, whether it was shown in the short window or kept the focus through it.

